+# Design
+
+## Context
+
+See proposal.md for motivation. Current state, verified in this checkout:
+
+- The `tools/call` branch of `_mcp_stdio()` in `plugins/graphhelm/hooks/task_handoff.py`
+  requires `params` to be a dict and rejects any key outside `{"name", "arguments"}`
+  (`set(params) - {...}`, raising `ValueError`, which the surrounding handler maps to JSON-RPC
+  `-32602 invalid request`). Only `params.get("name")` and `params.get("arguments")` are read at
+  dispatch.
+- The per-tool argument closure lives separately in `_mcp_tool_call()`: `set(arguments) - allowed`
+  (`allowed` = `{"recipientHost","recipientSessionId","handoffId"}` for `offer`, `{"offerId"}` for
+  `receive`/`status`). This is what makes the identity-override refusal work.
+- Frame bounds live in the read loop: a frame over `MAX_MCP_MESSAGE` (32 KiB) or without a
+  trailing newline answers one `-32600 invalid request`, closes the transport, and exits nonzero.
+- The two shipped copies `plugins/graphhelm/hooks/task_handoff.py` and
+  `plugins/graphhelm-codex-hooks/hooks/task_handoff.py` are byte-identical right now
+  (SHA-256 `2aeae452…f8c3` both). No build system copies or links them; they are literal file
+  duplicates.
+- `serverInfo` advertises version `0.1.11`; the existing stdio test asserts it equals
+  `plugin.json`'s `version` (currently `0.1.11`).
+- Per the MCP 2025-06-18 specification, `_meta` is standard metadata attached to JSON-RPC request
+  params (a map/object); compliant clients include it in `tools/call`, e.g.
+  `{"progressToken": ...}`.
+
+## Goals / Non-Goals
+
+**Goals:**
+
+- Accept a top-level `_meta` object in `tools/call` params and ignore it at dispatch.
+- Keep every other refusal working with the same wire behavior: other unknown `params` fields,
+  non-object `_meta`, identity overrides in `arguments`, oversized/unterminated frames.
+- Keep the two shipped copies byte-identical and make that contract observable from
+  `test_task_handoff.py`.
+
+**Non-Goals:**
+
+- No `_meta` processing: no progress notification replies, no relaying `_meta` anywhere.
+- No `_meta` accepted inside per-tool `arguments`.
+- No protocol version change (`protocolVersion` stays `2025-06-18`) and no plugin/serverInfo
+  version bump.
+- No changes to `initialize`, `ping`, `tools/list`, the argument schemas, or the CLI phases.
+
+## Decisions
+
+1. **Extend the `params` allow-list in `_mcp_stdio()`, with an explicit objectness check.**
+   Change the field check from `set(params) - {"name", "arguments"}` to
+   `set(params) - {"name", "arguments", "_meta"}`, and when `_meta` is present additionally require
+   `isinstance(params["_meta"], dict)` before dispatching. Rationale: the check stays where all
+   other `tools/call` param validation already is, so every failure path keeps raising `ValueError`
+   into the same handler and producing the same `-32602 invalid request` response shape. The
+   objectness check encodes the requested condition ("accept `_meta` when it is an object"): a
+   non-object `_meta` fails closed rather than being silently dropped. Alternative considered:
+   validating `_meta` inside `_mcp_tool_call()` — rejected because dispatch already ignores the
+   value and would then need its signature or a new channel to carry transport metadata.
+
+2. **`_meta` is accepted only at the top level of `params`.**
+   The per-tool argument closure in `_mcp_tool_call()` is untouched, so a `_meta` key inside
+   `arguments` is still an unsupported field and still returns an `isError: true` tool result.
+   Rationale: the request scopes the fix to fields of `params`, MCP places `_meta` on the request
+   params rather than the tool argument object, and leaving the argument closure untouched keeps
+   the identity-override refusal provably intact. Recorded assumption from the request wording:
+   "params has a field other than name and arguments" names the top-level params scope.
+
+3. **The `_meta` value is ignored after validation.**
+   Only `name` and `arguments` are read at dispatch; nothing echoes or interprets `_meta`. Its
+   size is bounded transitively by the existing 32 KiB frame bound, matching the adapter's
+   untrusted-input posture.
+
+4. **The Codex companion is updated as a byte copy, verified by hash.**
+   After editing `plugins/graphhelm/hooks/task_handoff.py`, copy exactly those bytes to
+   `plugins/graphhelm-codex-hooks/hooks/task_handoff.py` (no per-copy edits) and confirm with a
+   SHA-256 comparison of both files. Alternatives considered: a symlink or a build-time copy
+   step — rejected because the repository has no build system for the plugins and ships literal
+   file duplicates (changing the shipping mechanism is out of scope).
+
+5. **Byte-identity gets a committed test, in `test_task_handoff.py`.**
+   Add a test that compares the SHA-256 digests of the two checked-in files, resolving the
+   companion path relative to the test file
+   (`Path(__file__).resolve().parents[2] / "graphhelm-codex-hooks" / "hooks" / "task_handoff.py"`).
+   Rationale: byte-identity is the named shipping contract, and without this case the unittest run
+   would not observe a companion drift at all. The assertion message names both paths so a layout
+   move fails loudly instead of reading as a content mismatch.
+
+6. **No version bump in this change.**
+   The existing test pins `serverInfo.version` to `plugin.json`'s `version`; the request asks for
+   behavior parity, not a release. Bumping both together remains a separate release decision.
+
+## Risks / Trade-offs
+
+- [A client sends a very large `_meta`] → bounded by the existing 32 KiB frame limit; oversized
+  frames are already refused and close the transport.
+- [A future edit reads `_meta` at dispatch by accident] → dispatch reads only `name` and
+  `arguments`; the new tests assert identical results with and without `_meta`.
+- [The two copies drift again] → the new hash-comparison test fails the unittest run immediately,
+  in both lanes that run it.
+- [Non-object `_meta` stricter than needed] → compliant clients always send objects; the wire
+  consequence of either reading (drop vs reject) would have been identical, so the stricter
+  check costs nothing and matches "unknown fields fail closed".
+- [Params with `_meta` but no `name`] → dispatch proceeds as today with a missing name and
+  returns the existing `isError: true` "unknown handoff tool" result; behavior there is unchanged
+  by this design.
+
+## Migration Plan
+
+Pure additive widening of the accepted request set. Clients that do not send `_meta` behave
+exactly as before; clients sending any other unknown field are still refused. No state, storage,
+or schema migration. Rollback is reverting both task_handoff.py copies and the test additions in
+one step; no wire compatibility breaks because `protocolVersion` and the advertised tool surface
+are unchanged.
+
+## Open Questions
+
+- Whether the next *release* bumps the plugin/serverInfo version (e.g. to `0.1.12`) is a release
+  decision that does not affect this change's specs, approach, or task breakdown.
