+
+Add the tests first so the change has red and green observations. All tests follow the existing
+subprocess-over-stdio pattern of `test_mcp_stdio_lists_closed_tools_and_reaches_real_status_adapter`
+(same environment setup, `RuntimeHandler.requests` count as the no-side-effect witness).
+
+- [x] 1.1 Add a test that a `tools/call` with `params` of `{"name": "status", "arguments": {}, "_meta": {"progressToken": "pt-1"}}` returns a JSON-RPC result with `isError` false and text content, and that an otherwise identical call without `_meta` returns the same result (proves acceptance and that `_meta` is ignored at dispatch). Verify: running `python -m unittest test_task_handoff -v` from `plugins/graphhelm/hooks/` shows this test FAILING against the current code (the response is a `-32602` error), with every pre-existing test still passing.
+- [x] 1.2 Add a test that a `tools/call` with any top-level `params` field other than `name`, `arguments`, and `_meta` (for example `"host"`) still gets JSON-RPC `-32602`, and that a `tools/call` with `params._meta` set to a non-object (for example a string) also gets `-32602`, in both cases with `RuntimeHandler.requests` count unchanged (no Runtime side effects). Verify: `python -m unittest test_task_handoff -v` shows the test passing against the current code AND required to keep passing after the fix (it guards against over-accepting).
+- [x] 1.3 Add a test that `arguments` containing `_meta` (for example `{"_meta": {"progressToken": "pt-1"}}` for `status`, sent together with a valid `_meta` object at the `params` level) returns an `isError: true` tool result, and extend or mirror the existing identity-override case so `arguments` of `{"host": "claude"}` is exercised with a valid `_meta` object at the `params` level and still fails with no Runtime request. Verify: `python -m unittest test_task_handoff -v` shows the test passing against the current code AND required to keep passing after the fix.
+
+## 2. Implementation and companion sync
+
+- [x] 2.1 In `plugins/graphhelm/hooks/task_handoff.py`, in the `tools/call` branch of `_mcp_stdio()`: change the allow-list check from `set(params) - {"name", "arguments"}` to `set(params) - {"name", "arguments", "_meta"}`, and add a guard that a present `_meta` must be a JSON object (`isinstance(params["_meta"], dict)`), raising `ValueError` otherwise so both failures map to the existing `-32602 invalid request` response. Do not touch `_mcp_tool_call()`, the argument closure, the frame bounds, or any other method. Verify: `python -m unittest test_task_handoff -v` from `plugins/graphhelm/hooks/` passes with zero failures, including the new 1.1 test (now green) and the guard tests 1.2/1.3.
+- [x] 2.2 Copy the updated `plugins/graphhelm/hooks/task_handoff.py` byte-for-byte to `plugins/graphhelm-codex-hooks/hooks/task_handoff.py` (no per-copy edits). Verify: SHA-256 of both files is identical (for example `Get-FileHash` on both paths), confirming the byte-identical shipping contract.
+- [x] 2.3 Add a test to `plugins/graphhelm/hooks/test_task_handoff.py` asserting the two checked-in copies are byte-identical: compare SHA-256 digests of `Path(__file__)`'s sibling `task_handoff.py` and `Path(__file__).resolve().parents[2] / "graphhelm-codex-hooks" / "hooks" / "task_handoff.py"`, with the assertion failure message naming both paths so a layout move fails distinctly from a drift. Verify: `python -m unittest test_task_handoff -v` passes; temporarily append one byte to the companion copy and re-run to see this specific test fail, then restore the copy and re-run to green.
+
+## 3. Documentation and final observation
+
+- [x] 3.1 In `plugins/graphhelm/README.md`, update the stdio MCP paragraph (currently "Requests are bounded and unknown fields fail closed.") to state explicitly that a standard `_meta` object in `tools/call` params is accepted and ignored, while every other unknown field still fails closed. Verify: re-read the sentence against the implemented behavior in step 2.1 and against the spec scenarios in `specs/task-handoff-mcp/spec.md`; prose and behavior agree.
+- [x] 3.2 Run the complete test file one final time from a clean shell and record the outcome in the PR/delivery notes: `python -m unittest test_task_handoff -v` from `plugins/graphhelm/hooks/` must report every case (all pre-existing plus all new) passed, with the pre-existing oversized-frame and identity-override refusals unchanged. Verify: the final run exits 0 with zero failures, and the SHA-256 pair from task 2.2 is re-checked after all edits are in place.
